  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nThis Afternoon and Tonight:\n\nA stalled front stretches from near the Twin Cities eastward\nthrough north central Wi and across northern Lake Michigan this\nafternoon. North of this front gusty east winds are blowing\nacross the Northland. With increased low level moisture and flow\noff the lake, patchy drizzle and light rain are expected to\ncontinue. Starting to see more light showers develop over the \nArrowhead, as low level isentropic lift increases and a mid\nlevel jet moves overhead. Further to our west, more organized \nshowers and storms are ongoing across portions of ND. \n\nFor the rest of the afternoon and evening, showers will become\nmore widespread as a shortwave lifts through the region. Cannot\nrule out a thunderstorm or two, mainly from Brainerd eastward\nacross northwest WI. Instability parameters are meager, with\nlimited MUCAPE above a cap but in the hail growth zone. If a\nstronger storm does develop, small hail will be the main\nconcern. Additional rainfall amounts will be between\n0.10-0.50 inch, and dependent on where more persistent heavier \nrain falls. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday:\n\nUpper level flow becomes more zonal Tuesday, with showers \nending across the Arrowhead Tuesday morning. Low level moisture\nlingers across the area, and would not rule out a few light \nshowers around but for now will maintain a dry forecast for mid \nmorning and through the day. High pressure arrives Wednesday, \nkeeping conditions dry with clearing skies. Temperatures will be\nmuch more seasonable with highs in the 50s and 60s. Overnight \nlows will fall back into the 30s. \n\nThursday and Friday:\n\nNot many changes noted for the end of the week system. Guidance\ncontinues to be in better agreement with one low ejecting out \nof the Central Rockies starting Thursday morning, pushing\nmoisture northeastward toward the Upper Midwest. Another low\nwill be dropping south out of central Canada and will pick up\nthe Plains low somewhere over the Midwest. Although there is\nsome timing differences with the two systems, there is good\nagreement that moisture transport from the Plains will be mainly\nto our southeast. There will be enough moisture and forcing on\nthe north side of the Plains low for showers Thursday afternoon\nand evening, with the Canadian low currently lagging a bit\nbehind and mainly result in cold advection showers Thursday \nnight.\n\nWeekend:\n\nThe aforementioned lows will merge over the Great Lakes leading to a \nstrong pressure gradient setting up over the Northland. Gusty north \nwinds with ample cold air pouring in from the north will lead to a \nvery chilly Saturday morning with lows in the 20s. Dry air will also \ndominant most the region leading to mostly sunny skies and \nhighs continuing to be in the 50s and 60s. The dry and windy \nconditions may prompt fire weather concerns for Saturday.",
